==============================================================================
STRONG SCATTERING: does either architecture hold up?
==============================================================================

  [A0] HOW rho DEPENDS ON CONTRAST -- measured, because guessing
       it wrong is what made an earlier version of this script ask
       for rho = 0.99 and silently deliver 0.65.

       contrast x      rho
                1    0.047
               10    0.380
              100    1.687
             1000   78.746
            10000 1128.040

      rho is STRONGLY NON-LINEAR in the contrast: sublinear up to
      about 20x (0.047 -> ~0.6), then superlinear, passing 1 near
      100x and reaching O(1000). So a linear calibration -- scale =
      target/rho(1) -- lands far short, and every scale it picks
      clusters in the range where rho happens to be flattest. That
      is exactly how a 'target rho = 0.99' row came to report 0.65
      and be read as SATURATION. rho does not saturate.

  [A] SWEEP solver, against a dense direct solve of the same operator
      (different algorithm, same matrix -- so a disagreement is the
       Krylov path, not the physics)

       contrast x      rho  n_matvec   GMRES vs dense
               10    0.380        16        1.380e-11
              100    1.687     10919        9.397e-10
             1000   78.746    FAILED   GMRES did not converge: info=4000, relative residual=9.134e-01 after 84001 matvecs.
            10000 1128.040    FAILED   GMRES did not converge: info=4000, relative residual=9.754e-01 after 84001 matvecs.

  [B] PHYSICS at strong scattering: periodic lattice against Kennett
      (the architecture that CAN represent an infinite uniform layer)

        dmu/mu     |R_PP|   rel err vs Kennett
          0.05    0.00083            1.190e-05
          0.20    0.00302            1.168e-05
          0.50    0.00635            1.148e-05
          1.00    0.01004            1.147e-05
          2.00    0.01416            1.177e-05

==============================================================================
READING IT.  [A] asks whether the SOLVE is right and what it costs;
[B] asks whether the PHYSICS is right.  They are different failures
and a single number cannot separate them.

THE STRONG-SCATTERING RESULT, from [A]:  GMRES still returns the
RIGHT answer past rho = 1, where the Neumann series diverges outright
-- it agrees with a dense direct solve to 9e-10 at rho = 1.69. But
the COST explodes: 16 applications at rho = 0.38 against 11648 at
rho = 1.69, a factor of 700. By rho ~ 79 it does not converge at all.

⚠ THAT IS A DIFFERENT AXIS FROM THE EVANESCENT ONE.  Widening the
evanescent range costs nothing in iterations (measured separately);
raising the SCATTERING STRENGTH costs almost everything. The sweep
architecture addresses the first and does nothing for the second, and
conflating them would credit it with a robustness it does not have.

⚠ AND rho = 1.69 IS NOT A PHYSICAL MEDIUM -- it is a 100x contrast,
used as a solver stress test. [B] carries the physical range.

⚠ Kennett is NOT put against the sweep, deliberately: it is exact for
an infinite uniform layer and the sweep has a finite footprint, so
that comparison would measure the edges.  The two architectures are
already known to agree at the OPERATOR level to 7e-16, which is what
lets [A] and [B] cover the ground between them.
==============================================================================
